[0.011, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did signups drop in mid-June 2026?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 81s over 9 steps, 33,670 tokens, 19 observations gathered, 15 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups genuinely dropped by about 50% (from ~214.5/day to ~107/day) starting 2026-06-16/17, and this lower level has persisted through the end of the observed data on 2026-06-29.  [posthog.event_trend 9020e0ae]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups dropped ~50% in a sustained, real level shift  (high)\r\n    - Daily signups averaged about 214.5/day before June 16 and fell to about 107/day after, a -50.1% change, and this shift is statistically distinguishable from ordinary day-to-day noise.  [posthog.event_trend 9020e0ae]\r\n    - The lower rate (r"]
[0.000, "o", "oughly 93-109 signups/day) persisted through the end of the observed window on 2026-06-29, so this is a sustained shift rather than a brief dip.  [posthog.event_trend 9020e0ae]\r\n  No source-level breakdown was available to identify which segment or channel  (low)\r\n    - A breakdown of signups by $current_url returned the same aggregate daily series with no per-URL split, so the drop could not be decomposed by page or channel from this data.  [posthog.event_trend 268ddb9d]\r\n  No code, deploy, or discussion record  (medium)\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/product between June 1 and June 20.  [github.commits 4e946f2d, github.commits f67ba6f5]\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/marketing-site (June 10-20) or acme/mobile (June 1-20) either.  [github.commits c26ab3ed, github.commits b8f00adb]\r\n    - No deployments to any environment (web-preview, web-live) were recorded for acme/product in the relevant window, and no pull requests were found for the repo at all.  [github.deployment_history f88b3c4a, github.d"]
[0.000, "o", "eployment_history 845f5c2d, github.recent_prs bc1c6332]\r\n    - No PostHog annotations or feature flags mention signup, and no Slack messages or decisions matching \"signup drop\", \"signups\", or \"incident\" were found around this period.  [posthog.annotations 86446836, posthog.feature_flags e1ee575a, slack.search_messages 6518b619, slack.search_messages 15f55297, slack.find_decision f3f7bed1, slack.search_messages fac10b71]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |█▇█▇█▇████▇▇▇▇▇▇▁▁▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▂▁▂▁|  range 93–229\r\n                        2026-06-01 … 2026-06-29\r\n\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |▇▇▆▇▇██▇█▇▇▇▆█▇▇██▇█▇▇▇████▇▇▇▇▇▇▁▁▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▂▁▂▁|  range 93–230\r\n                        2026-05-15 … 2026-06-29\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n---------------"]
[0.000, "o", "---------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [inconclusive] A code deploy to acme/product around June 16-17 caused the signup drop.\r\n      No commits or deployments were recorded for acme/product in this window at all, which rules out a deploy-based explanation as the visible cause, but it is equally consistent with the deploy/commit history for this repo not being tracked by the connected tool for this window, so this is inconclusive rather than a clean contradiction.\r\n  [inconclusive] A marketing site or mobile app change caused the drop.\r\n      No commits appear in acme/marketing-site or acme/mobile in the window either, but as with the product repo this could reflect incomplete visibility rather than a true absence of activity.\r\n  [contradicted] The drop was noticed and discussed internally (e.g. as an incident) around the time it began.\r\n      Multiple targeted Slack searches for signup drop, signups, incident, and decisions about signup around the June 14-25 window all returned zer"]
[0.000, "o", "o matches, indicating no visible internal discussion of this event in the connected Slack workspace.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Investigate signup instrumentation and upstream sources (ad platforms, referral channels, server-side signup event emission) directly, since no cause is visible in code, deploys, or team communication for this tenant's connected tools.\r\n      why: The drop is real and sustained (-50.1%, since 2026-06-16) but every internal source that could name a cause — commits, deployments, PRs, annotations, feature flags, and Slack — returned empty for the relevant window, leaving no named artefact to point to.\r\n      [posthog.event_trend 9020e0ae, github.deployment_history f88b3c4a, slack.search_messages 6518b619]\r\n  P2 Add a breakdown property (channel/source) to the signup event or confirm one exists, since $current_url returned no segmentation data.\r\n      why: Without a working breakdown, it is impossible to te"]
[0.000, "o", "ll whether the drop is uniform or concentrated in one channel, which is the next step needed to narrow the cause.\r\n      [posthog.event_trend 268ddb9d]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The event breakdown by $current_url returned no per-URL data, so the drop could not be attributed to a specific page, channel, or segment; the cause could be channel-specific but this cannot be seen from here.\r\n  - GitHub commit, deployment, and PR queries all returned empty for the relevant window across all three repos; this could mean no activity occurred, or that the connector does not have history for this period/repo combination. Absence of a Git-based explanation should not be read as certainty that no code change occurred.\r\n  - Slack searches used specific keyword queries (\"signup drop\", \"signups\", \"incident\"); a relevant discussion using different wording would not have surfaced.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the "]
[0.000, "o", "cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 2 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- executive_summary[2]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: Signups dropped sharply (roughly 50%) starting on 2026-06-17 and remained lower t\r\n- findings[2].claims[3]: The feature_flags query used an empty search string rather than 'signup', so it doesn't actually show flags were checked for signup mentions; other searches match as claimed.\r\n  - 3 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The data establishes a clear, statistically distinguishable level shift in signups on 2026-06-16, but every search for a candidate cause (commits, deploys, PRs, feature flags, Slack"]
[0.000, "o", " discussion, annotations) returned zero rows across all relevant repos and channels. This shows nobody has found or logged an explanatory change, not that no cause exists, so the movement is well-documented but its cause is absent from these observations. What would settle it: A dated deploy/config/marketing change around 2026-06-16 across all relevant repos/systems (commits, PRs, deploys all returned empty); A comparable series unaffected by the change to rule out instrumentation/tracking issues; Confirmation that the signup event capture itself wasn't altered (SDK/tracking change) around 2026-06-16; Any internal or external record (Slac\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - github.commits and github.recent_prs returned zero rows for acme/product, acme/marketing-site, and acme/mobile across the investigation window; this is presented as an observation, not proof that no code changed, since it may reflect connector coverage rather than actual repo"]
[0.000, "o", " history.\r\n  - The $current_url breakdown query for the signup event returned an unsegmented result, so no per-channel or per-page split of the drop is available.\r\n  - 13 of the 15 cited observation(s) returned no results (github.commits, github.deployment_history, github.recent_prs, posthog.annotations, posthog.feature_flags, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  4e946f2d  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  f67ba6f5  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  c26ab3ed  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  b8f00adb  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  f88b3c4a  github.deployment_history  eval://github__deployment_history\r\n  845f5c2d  github.deployment_history  eval://github__deployment_history\r\n  bc1c6332  github.recent_prs  eval://github__recent_prs\r\n  86446836  posthog.annotations  eval://p"]
[0.000, "o", "osthog__annotations\r\n  268ddb9d  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  9020e0ae  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  e1ee575a  posthog.feature_flags  eval://posthog__feature_flags\r\n  f3f7bed1  slack.find_decision  eval://slack__find_decision\r\n  fac10b71  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  6518b619  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  15f55297  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n"]
